{
 "id": "with-cat-like-tread",
 "version": 1,
 "reviewed": true,
 "title": "With Cat-like Tread",
 "description": "The pirates' stealthy march from Gilbert and Sullivan's The Pirates of Penzance, for 2 to 4 singers: the end of the Act II reprise, where the pirates sing \"Come, friends, who plough the sea\" over the police's hushed \"tarantara\", then both choruses together, very loud, \"With cat-like tread\". The tune climbs to a held top E near the end. Sullivan wrote it for two men's choruses; here, as mixed choirs sing it, the women take the pirates and the men the police.",
 "language": "en",
 "difficulty": "intermediate",
 "genre": [
  "musical theatre",
  "comic opera"
 ],
 "style": "straight",
 "tempo": 90,
 "key": "A",
 "timeSignature": "4/4",
 "pickupBeats": 2,
 "credits": {
  "words": "W. S. Gilbert (1879)",
  "tune": "Arthur Sullivan (1879)",
  "arrangement": "Arthur Sullivan (1879), adapted by Stackapella"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: Gilbert died 1911",
  "tune": "public domain: Sullivan died 1900",
  "arrangement": "Sullivan's Pirates and Police choruses (public domain), for mixed voices in A: the end of the reprise only, with breaths written in for beginners, the alto written under \"Come, friends\" and revoiced from letter E so 2 and 3 singers keep each chord's third (but for the E7 on \"we\", where the alto's seventh, D, falling to C-sharp on \"feel\", is kept instead), and the police's quick \"ra\"s halved. Accepted exceptions (singable.py): Sullivan's own notes, checked on the Chappell scan (p. 152): the tune's top E held through \"way\" and \"we\", about 5 seconds at the top of the beginner soprano range; and the parallel octaves into the last chord, the tune falling from E to A on \"we feel.\" while the bass falls from E to A an octave and more below, are in the print, where the pirates' top line falls A to D over the police's A to D (in D, a fourth higher).",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "W. S. Gilbert and Arthur Sullivan, The Pirates of Penzance, vocal score (Chappell & Co., London, plate 23731), Act II No. 12, the full-chorus reprise of \"With cat-like tread\", pp. 150 to 152; this song keeps its last part, from \"Come, friends\" (p. 151) to the end of the voices (p. 152), in D for men's double chorus and written here in A; read from the scan at archive.org/details/vocalscoreofpira00sull (pages n157 to n159)"
 },
